## Root cause

The report recurred because this branch predates SRCH09, and it kept re-creating the poison:

1. **Live-config poisoning (the recurring trigger).** 19 test binaries ran under the real app name `Arora`, so every `make check` on this lane wrote QSettings into the user's live `~/.config/Arora/Arora.conf` — including `urlloading/searchEngineFallback=false`. With the opt-out active, `test query` produced `http://test query` (invalid even in TolerantMode — verified) which `loadUrl` dropped silently → dead Enter on the real browser. Master's tests were already isolated; rust-work's weren't.
2. **No engine rescue.** `ToolbarSearch::searchNow` early-returned on a null engine, and both omnibox fallbacks emitted `http://<input>` when engine resolution failed — invalid for multi-word input.
3. **Opt-out emitted unparsable guesses** — the Rust classifier's `fallback_verdict` emitted `navigate: http://two words` without validating, and the Qt path did the same.

## Fixes ported (SRCH09-equivalent)

- `autotests/qtest_arora.h` — each test binary's settings scope is renamed to its basename after `BrowserApplication` resets the app name.
- `OpenSearchManager::searchUrlForEngine()` — engine → bundled DuckDuckGo → hardcoded endpoint; used by `searchNow` and both omnibox fallbacks.
- `fallback_verdict` (Rust) + `fallbackUrl` (C++) — bare-http guess only when it parses, else search.
- `TabWidget::loadUrl` — warns on invalid URLs instead of silently dropping.

## Verification

- `tst_tabwidget` 16/16 — new `omniboxEnterDispatch` (real Enter with completer popup open → engine search; "Search %engine" suggestion pick → search), plus broken-engine-rescue and opt-out-multi-word legs in `omnibox`.
- `tst_toolbarsearch` 10/10 — new broken-engine Enter leg emits the DDG rescue URL.
- `cargo test omnibox` 11/11.
- `make check` gates all PASS (warnings/no-rust build, supplychain, rustcoverage); suite green except `tst_dialogs::clearSiteData` — a documented pre-existing localStorage-flush timing flake that passes standalone.
